# 63: inside `hide()`, the Go port does not resolve a list spread's
# template computation; TypeScript does. An ADR-001 parity break with
# OPPOSITE EXIT CODES on a document neither port reports as wrong.
#
# run: node ../../../ts/bin/aontu.js -c hide-blocks-spread-compute-in-go.aon
# run: aontu-go -c hide-blocks-spread-compute-in-go.aon
#
# expected: one answer
#
# actual canon (TypeScript) -- mark absorbed, template applied:
#   {"src":[{"n":"a"}],"u":{"rows":[&:{"o":.n},{"n":"a","o":"a"}]}}
# actual canon (Go) -- mark kept, `o` still the UNRESOLVED `.n`:
#   {"src":[{"n":"a"}],"u":{"rows":hide([&:{"o":.n},{"n":"a","o":.n}])}}
#
# actual generation (TypeScript): {"src":[{"n":"a"}],"u":{}}   exit 0
#                                 -- hidden, so correctly dropped
# actual generation (Go):         [aontu/mapval_no_gen] at $.u.rows
#                                                             exit 1
#
# BOUNDARY, probed. The break needs BOTH the mark and a template that
# COMPUTES from a relative reference:
#   [&: {o: .n}] & $.src            without hide()  -> both ports agree
#   hide([{o: "a"}])                literal list    -> both ports agree
#   hide([&: {o: "K"}] & $.src)     constant template -> both agree
#   hide([&: {o: .n}] & $.src)      this file       -> DIVERGE
#
# WHY IT MATTERS. `hide()` around a staged intermediate is the natural
# way to keep scaffolding out of generated output, and staging is
# required because `pick` over an inline spread expression does not
# settle. So the obvious spelling of a code-generation transform --
# compute the lines into a hidden key, project them with `pick` --
# works in TypeScript and refuses in Go. use-cases/15-code-generation
# had to drop `hide()` for exactly this reason; its check.sh asserts
# byte-identical output from both ports and would have shipped a
# TypeScript-only transform without that assertion.
src: [{n: "a"}]
u: {rows: hide([&: {o: .n}] & $.src)}
